{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjzHwk26a25m5gTCZ56","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking about what happens when they run `gh pr merge --auto --squash` on a GitHub PR that has branch protection requiring two approvals, but only one approval is currently present. They want to know if it waits or errors out.\n\nThis is a question about GitHub CLI behavior and GitHub's branch protection rules. Let me think about what I know:\n\nThe `--auto` flag in `gh pr merge` enables \"auto-merge\" for the PR. When auto-merge is enabled with branch protection rules that require approvals:\n- If the branch protection rule requires 2 approvals and only 1 is present, the PR won't be eligible to merge immediately\n- With `--auto`, GitHub will queue the merge to happen automatically once all conditions are met (in this case, once the second approval comes in)\n- So it shouldn't error out - it should queue and wait\n\nHowever, I should be careful here because GitHub CLI behavior can be specific and this is the kind of detail that changes between versions. The user is asking a practical implementation question where the answer matters for their workflow.\n\nI should be honest about what I'm confident about:\n- `--auto` enables auto-merge\n- It should queue the merge when conditions aren't met, rather than error\n- But the exact behavior might depend on GitHub CLI version or specific configuration\n\nI don't think I need to invoke any tools for this - it's a straightforward question about how a tool works. I should give a direct answer based on what auto-merge does, and acknowledge any uncertainty about edge cases.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6833,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6833},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"3221b04c-c6a4-424b-9641-fed2d2e107ca","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:46:56.562Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjzGwxmKa9USjdmm74N"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjzHwk26a25m5gTCZ56","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`gh pr merge --auto --squash` enables **auto-merge** — it queues the merge to happen automatically once all branch protection requirements are met. It doesn't error out.\n\nSo in your scenario (2 approvals required, 1 present):\n- The command succeeds and queues the PR\n- GitHub watches for the second approval to come in\n- Once it does, the merge happens automatically with squash\n\nThe PR won't merge until all conditions pass. You can see queued auto-merges in the PR UI (there's a banner showing auto-merge is pending), and you can cancel it with `gh pr merge --disable-auto` if needed.\n\nIf branch protection *rejects* the merge outright (e.g., required status checks failing), auto-merge will still queue it and keep trying, or fail if the rejection is permanent and GitHub decides the condition can't be satisfied. But \"not enough approvals yet\" is a temporary state that auto-merge is designed to handle."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6833,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6833},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"70e27dee-7703-410d-87b6-e81ecde54bd0","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:46:59.205Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjzGwxmKa9USjdmm74N"}
